<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Adaptive launcher icon, round mask variant. Chapter 30.

    `<background>` now points at @drawable/ic_launcher_background — a gradient
    shape — where it used to point at @color/ic_launcher_background, a flat
    #FFFFFF. That colour resource has been deleted rather than left unused:
    keeping it would have meant a colour and a drawable sharing one name, which
    Android permits and no reader should have to disambiguate.

    Why it changed: the foreground is a white sparkle on transparency, so a
    white background made the whole icon invisible. The app drawer showed
    CartWise as an empty white circle. See ../drawable/ic_launcher_background.xml.

    `<monochrome>` is the Android 13+ themed-icon layer. It reuses the same
    foreground because that asset is already a solid silhouette on transparency,
    which is exactly what the themed pipeline wants — it recolours the opaque
    pixels and discards the rest. Without it, a user with themed icons switched
    on gets CartWise rendered in full colour beside a uniformly tinted home
    screen.
-->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <background android:drawable="@drawable/ic_launcher_background"/>
    <foreground android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground"/>
    <monochrome android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground"/>
</adaptive-icon>
